    "tabs": {
      "history": {
        "title": "Firearm Origin...",
        "content": "The Ruger 10/22 is a semi-automatic rimfire rifle introduced in 1964 by Sturm, Ruger & Co. Designed as an affordable, reliable, and easy-to-use firearm, it quickly became one of the most popular .22 caliber rifles in the world. Its simple blowback action and detachable rotary magazine contributed to its reputation for dependable performance and smooth operation.\n\nThe rifle’s innovative 10-round rotary magazine, which fits flush with the stock, was a defining feature that set it apart from competing designs. This system provided reliable feeding while maintaining a compact profile, making it ideal for hunting, target shooting, and training.\n\nOver the decades, the 10/22 has gained a massive following due to its modularity. A wide aftermarket ecosystem allows users to customize nearly every component, including stocks, barrels, triggers, and optics. This flexibility has made it a favorite platform for hobbyists and competitive shooters alike.\n\nThe 10/22 is commonly used for small game hunting, recreational shooting, and firearms training due to its low recoil and inexpensive ammunition. Its durability and ease of maintenance further contribute to its widespread appeal.\n\nToday, the Ruger 10/22 remains one of the most successful and recognizable rimfire rifles ever produced, valued for its reliability, versatility, and long-standing role in American shooting culture.",
