    "tabs": {
      "history": {
        "title": "Firearm Origin...",
        "content": "The Steyr Modelo 1912 Mauser is a bolt-action rifle originally produced for export contracts, most notably for South American nations such as Chile and Argentina. Manufactured by Steyr in Austria, these rifles were based on the proven Mauser 98 action, known for its strength, reliability, and controlled-round feeding system.\n\nThe Modelo 1912 was chambered in various calibers depending on the purchasing nation, and featured high-quality machining and durable construction. Like other Mauser-pattern rifles, it utilized a five-round internal magazine loaded via stripper clips and was designed for military service under harsh conditions.\n\nIn later years, many of these rifles were converted to fire modern cartridges such as 7.62×51mm NATO (.308 Winchester). These conversions were often performed for surplus resale, allowing older military rifles to remain functional with widely available ammunition. While practical, such modifications sometimes altered original specifications and collector value.\n\nThe Mauser 98 action itself became one of the most influential firearm designs of the 20th century, serving as the basis for countless military and civilian rifles worldwide. Its strength and reliability made it especially suitable for high-pressure cartridges.\n\nToday, converted Modelo 1912 rifles represent a blend of historical craftsmanship and modern utility, often appreciated both as shooters and as examples of early 20th-century European arms manufacturing.",
        "wikipedia": {
          "title": "",
          "content": "",
          "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Springfield_M1903"
        },
        "gunDigest": {
          "title": "Mauser: The Most Important Rifle",
          "content": "",
          "url": "https://gundigest.com/more/classic-guns/mauser-the-most-important-rifle"
