      "history": {
        "title": "Firearm Origin...",
        "content": "The Karabiner 98k (K98k) was the standard service rifle of German forces during World War II. Developed from earlier Mauser designs, it was officially adopted in 1935 and remained in widespread use throughout the conflict. Chambered in 7.92×57mm Mauser, the rifle was known for its strength, accuracy, and reliability under battlefield conditions.\n\nThe K98k utilized the Mauser 98 bolt-action system, which featured controlled-round feeding and dual front locking lugs. This design provided exceptional durability and made it one of the strongest bolt-action systems ever produced. The rifle was typically fed from a five-round internal magazine loaded via stripper clips.\n\nCompared to earlier Gewehr 98 rifles, the K98k was shorter and more maneuverable, making it better suited for modern infantry tactics. It was widely issued to German soldiers and also served as the basis for sniper variants equipped with optical sights.\n\nProduction was carried out by multiple factories, with wartime manufacturing increasingly simplified to meet demand. Despite these changes, the rifle maintained a reputation for precision and ruggedness.\n\nThe K98k remains one of the most iconic military rifles of the 20th century and has had a lasting influence on both military and sporting rifle design.",
        "wikipedia": {
          "title": "",
          "content": "",
          "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karabiner_98k"
        },
        "gunDigest": {
          "title": "",
          "content": "",
          "url": "https://gundigest.com/?s=Mauser%20K98%20(1937)%20Gun%20Digest"
        }
      },
