    "tabs": {
      "history": {
        "title": "Firearm Origin...",
        "content": "The Mosin-Nagant M91/30 is a bolt-action rifle that served as the primary infantry weapon of Soviet forces during World War II. It is an updated version of the original Mosin-Nagant rifle adopted in 1891, redesigned for simplified mass production and improved battlefield utility.\n\nChambered in 7.62×54R, the rifle features a rugged bolt-action mechanism with a five-round internal magazine loaded via stripper clips. Its design prioritized reliability and ease of manufacture, making it well suited for the large-scale production demands of wartime.\n\nThe M91/30 was widely issued to Soviet troops and also served as the basis for sniper variants equipped with optical sights. Its long barrel contributed to strong ballistic performance and effective range.\n\nDespite its relatively simple construction, the rifle proved highly durable in harsh environments, including extreme cold and battlefield conditions. Millions were produced during the war.\n\nToday, the Mosin-Nagant remains one of the most widely produced military rifles in history and is valued for its historical significance and rugged reliability.",
        "wikipedia": {
          "title": "Mosin–Nagant",
          "content": "The Mosin–Nagant is a five-shot, bolt-action, internal magazine–fed military rifle.",
