      "history": {
        "title": "Firearm Origin...",
        "content": "The Dutch Beaumont Model 1896 is a bolt-action rifle developed during a transitional period in firearms history as militaries shifted from black powder to smokeless powder cartridges. It evolved from earlier Beaumont-Vitali designs and incorporated improvements for modern ammunition and repeating fire capability.\n\nChambered in 6.5×53mmR, the rifle used a bolt-action system combined with a detachable magazine, reflecting advancements in late 19th-century military rifle design. While not as advanced as Mauser-pattern rifles, it represented an important step forward in military small arms.\n\nThe Beaumont system was relatively simple and reliable, though it lacked some of the strength and refinement of later designs. It saw service with Dutch forces in both Europe and colonial territories, including the Dutch East Indies.\n\nAs firearm technology progressed, the rifle was eventually replaced by more modern Mauser-based designs. However, it remains an important example of transitional military firearm development.\n\nToday, the Dutch Beaumont m.1896 is primarily of historical interest and is appreciated by collectors as a representation of early bolt-action evolution.",
        "wikipedia": {
          "title": "",
          "content": "",
          "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:Search?search=Dutch%20Beaumont%20m.1896"
        },
        "gunDigest": {
          "title": "",
          "content": "",
          "url": "https://gundigest.com/?s=Dutch%20Beaumont%20m.1896%20Gun%20Digest"
        }
      },
