      "history": {
        "title": "Firearm Origin...",
        "content": "The AR-15 is a lightweight, gas-operated semi-automatic rifle originally developed in the late 1950s and later produced by numerous manufacturers, including Windham Weaponry. Based on Eugene Stoner’s original design, the AR-15 platform has become one of the most widely used and adaptable rifle systems in the world.\n\nThe rifle operates using a direct impingement gas system, in which gas is routed from the barrel to cycle the bolt carrier group. This design contributes to reduced recoil and a straight-line stock configuration that improves control and accuracy.\n\nWindham Weaponry produces AR-15 rifles known for quality manufacturing and adherence to established specifications. The platform’s modular design allows users to customize nearly every component, including barrels, triggers, handguards, and optics.\n\nChambered most commonly in 5.56×45mm NATO or .223 Remington, the AR-15 is used for sport shooting, hunting, and defensive applications. Its ergonomic layout and low recoil make it accessible to a wide range of shooters.\n\nToday, the AR-15 remains one of the most influential firearm designs in modern history, valued for its versatility and adaptability.",
        "wikipedia": {
