    "tabs": {
      "history": {
        "title": "Firearm Origin...",
        "content": "The Lee–Enfield No.5 Mk I, commonly known as the \"Jungle Carbine,\" was a shortened and lightened variant of the No.4 rifle developed during World War II. Introduced in 1944, it was intended for use in jungle and airborne operations where a more compact and maneuverable rifle was required.\n\nThe rifle featured a shorter barrel, a distinctive flash hider, and lightening cuts to reduce weight. A rubber recoil pad was added to improve shooter comfort, though recoil remained sharper than the standard No.4 rifle.\n\nWhile the Jungle Carbine offered improved handling in confined environments, it became associated with a phenomenon known as \"wandering zero,\" where accuracy could shift unpredictably. This issue limited its long-term adoption.\n\nThe No.5 saw service primarily in Southeast Asia and other tropical regions during the final stages of World War II.\n\nToday, the Jungle Carbine is a distinctive and collectible variant of the Lee–Enfield family, appreciated for its unique design and specialized role.",
        "wikipedia": {
          "title": "",
          "content": "",
          "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:Search?search=Enfield%20Jungle%20Carbine"
