      "history": {
        "title": "Firearm Origin...",
        "content": "The MAS-49/56 is a semi-automatic service rifle developed by France and adopted in 1956 as the culmination of earlier MAS rifle designs. Chambered in 7.5×54mm French, it was designed to be compact, reliable, and versatile for modern infantry use.\n\nThe rifle uses a direct impingement gas system, allowing for reliable cycling with relatively few moving parts. It features a detachable box magazine, integral grenade-launching capability, and robust iron sights.\n\nThe MAS-49/56 saw service in conflicts such as the Algerian War and remained in use by French forces into the 1970s. Some rifles were later converted to 7.62×51mm NATO for export or civilian markets.\n\nCompared to earlier battle rifles, the MAS-49/56 was relatively lightweight and compact, making it well suited for a variety of roles.\n\nToday, it is valued for its unique design and as a representation of post-war European military firearms development.",
        "wikipedia": {
          "title": "",
          "content": "",
          "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Springfield_M1903"
