    "tabs": {
      "history": {
        "title": "Firearm Origin...",
        "content": "The SVT-40 is a semi-automatic battle rifle developed by the Soviet Union and introduced in 1940 as an improvement over the earlier SVT-38. Designed by Fedor Tokarev, it was intended to increase the firepower of Soviet infantry.\n\nChambered in 7.62×54R, the SVT-40 uses a gas-operated system with a tilting bolt. It features a detachable 10-round magazine and can also be loaded using stripper clips, providing flexibility in the field.\n\nThe rifle offered a higher rate of fire than traditional bolt-action rifles, but it required more maintenance and training to operate effectively. As a result, production eventually shifted back toward simpler designs like the Mosin-Nagant.\n\nDespite these challenges, the SVT-40 saw extensive use during World War II and influenced later semi-automatic rifle designs, including those developed by Germany.\n\nToday, the SVT-40 is valued as an early example of semi-automatic military rifle development and remains an important part of Soviet firearms history.",
        "wikipedia": {
          "title": "SVT-40 on Wikipedia",
          "content": "",
          "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SVT-40"
        },
        "gunDigest": {
          "title": "The SVT-40: Soviet Russia’s Battle Rifle",
          "content": "",
