      "history": {
        "title": "Firearm Origin...",
        "content": "The FN-49 is a semi-automatic rifle developed by Fabrique Nationale in Belgium in the late 1940s. Designed by Dieudonné Saive, it represents a transitional step between traditional bolt-action rifles and modern battle rifles.\n\nChambered in a variety of calibers depending on the contract, including 7.92×57mm Mauser and .30-06 Springfield, the FN-49 uses a gas-operated system with a tilting bolt. It features a fixed magazine loaded via stripper clips.\n\nThe rifle was adopted by several countries, including Egypt, Venezuela, and Luxembourg, and saw service in various post-war conflicts.\n\nThe FN-49 was known for its robust construction and accuracy, though it was soon overshadowed by more modern designs such as the FN FAL.\n\nToday, the FN-49 is highly regarded by collectors as a well-crafted and historically significant semi-automatic rifle from the early Cold War era.",
        "wikipedia": {
