    "tabs": {
      "history": {
        "title": "Firearm Origin...",
        "content": "The Hakim rifle is a semi-automatic battle rifle produced in Egypt during the 1950s, based on the Swedish AG-42 Ljungman design. Chambered in 7.92×57mm Mauser, it was adapted to use surplus German ammunition following World War II.\n\nThe rifle uses a direct impingement gas system and features a detachable magazine that is typically loaded using stripper clips. It is known for its distinctive large size and powerful recoil.\n\nThe Hakim was issued to Egyptian forces and saw service in regional conflicts during the mid-20th century. Its robust construction made it reliable, though its weight and recoil were notable characteristics.\n\nA unique feature of the Hakim is its adjustable gas system, allowing the rifle to function with varying ammunition types.\n\nToday, the Hakim rifle is appreciated for its distinctive design and as an example of Cold War-era adaptation of earlier European firearm technology.",
        "wikipedia": {
          "title": "",
          "content": "",
          "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:Search?search=Egyptian%20Hakim"
