    "tabs": {
      "history": {
        "title": "Firearm Origin...",
        "content": "The MAS-36 is a bolt-action service rifle developed and produced by France in the late 1930s as a modern replacement for earlier Lebel and Berthier rifles. Officially adopted in 1936, it was designed to be simple, rugged, and easy to manufacture, reflecting lessons learned from World War I.\n\nChambered in 7.5×54mm French, the MAS-36 features a rear-locking bolt with dual lugs and a short, sturdy receiver. Unlike many contemporary rifles, it was intentionally built without a manual safety, as French military doctrine emphasized carrying the rifle with an empty chamber until ready to fire.\n\nThe rifle incorporates several distinctive design elements, including a turned-down bolt handle, a hooded front sight, and a permanently mounted spike bayonet stored beneath the barrel. Its simple construction minimized machining complexity and improved reliability in field conditions.\n\nAlthough adopted shortly before World War II, the MAS-36 saw limited early wartime use due to France’s rapid defeat in 1940. Production resumed after the war, and the rifle remained in service through conflicts such as the First Indochina War and the Algerian War.\n\nDespite being a bolt-action rifle in an era increasingly dominated by semi-automatic designs, the MAS-36 proved durable and effective in harsh environments.\n\nToday, the MAS-36 is valued for its distinctive design, robust construction, and its role as France’s last standard-issue bolt-action service rifle.",
        "wikipedia": {
          "title": "",
          "content": "",
          "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:Search?search=MAS%20M1936"
        },
        "gunDigest": {
