      "history": {
        "title": "Firearm Origin...",
        "content": "The M1 Garand is a semi-automatic service rifle that was the standard U.S. infantry weapon during World War II and the Korean War. Designed by John Garand, it was officially adopted in 1936.\n\nChambered in .30-06 Springfield, the rifle uses a gas-operated system and is fed by an eight-round en bloc clip. It was the first semi-automatic rifle to be widely issued to a nation’s infantry.\n\nThe Garand provided a significant advantage in firepower over bolt-action rifles, allowing soldiers to fire more rapidly without sacrificing accuracy.\n\nPost-war production rifles, including those from the 1950s, continued to serve in military and reserve roles.\n\nToday, the M1 Garand is one of the most iconic American military rifles and remains popular among collectors and competitive shooters.",
