      "history": {
        "title": "Firearm Origin...",
        "content": "The early-production M1 Garand represents one of the first large-scale fieldings of a semi-automatic service rifle. Adopted in 1936, rifles produced around 1941 were among those used during the United States’ entry into World War II.\n\nChambered in .30-06 Springfield, the Garand uses a gas-operated rotating bolt system and is fed by an eight-round en bloc clip. Its semi-automatic operation provided a major advantage in rate of fire over bolt-action rifles.\n\nEarly war rifles featured milled components and high-quality finishes before wartime production simplifications were introduced.\n\nThe Garand saw extensive service across multiple theaters of World War II and became one of the most respected infantry weapons of the conflict.\n\nToday, early-production Garands are especially valued for their historical significance and craftsmanship.",
