      "history": {
        "title": "Firearm Origin...",
        "content": "The M1 Carbine is a lightweight semi-automatic rifle developed during World War II to provide a compact firearm for support troops, officers, and paratroopers. Introduced in 1941, it was produced in large numbers by several manufacturers.\n\nChambered in .30 Carbine, the rifle uses a gas-operated system and is fed from detachable box magazines. It is significantly lighter and more compact than the M1 Garand.\n\nThe M1 Carbine was designed to bridge the gap between pistols and full-size rifles, offering greater accuracy and range than handguns while remaining easy to carry.\n\nBy 1944, production was at its peak, and the rifle saw widespread use in multiple theaters of World War II.\n\nToday, the M1 Carbine remains popular for its historical significance, light recoil, and handy size.",
