    "tabs": {
      "history": {
        "title": "Firearm Origin...",
        "content": "The Remington Model 700 is a bolt-action rifle introduced in 1962 and one of the most widely produced and influential sporting rifles in modern history. Designed as a successor to earlier Remington models, it was engineered for accuracy, affordability, and ease of mass production.\n\nThe Model 700 utilizes a push-feed bolt-action system with dual opposed locking lugs and a recessed bolt face that fully encloses the cartridge head. This design contributes to strength, consistency, and reliable ignition.\n\nA key feature of the Model 700 is its cylindrical receiver, which simplifies manufacturing while providing a rigid platform for accuracy. The rifle has become a popular choice for both hunters and precision shooters.\n\nChambered in a wide variety of calibers, the Model 700 is used for applications ranging from varmint hunting to long-range precision shooting. It has also served as the basis for military and law enforcement sniper rifles, including the M24 and M40.\n\nThe platform’s widespread adoption has led to a massive aftermarket, allowing extensive customization of stocks, triggers, barrels, and optics.\n\nToday, the Remington Model 700 remains one of the most recognizable and widely used bolt-action rifles, valued for its accuracy, versatility, and influence on modern rifle design.",
        "wikipedia": {
          "title": "Remington Model 700",
          "content": "The Remington Model 700 is a bolt-action rifle introduced in 1962 and one of the most widely produced and influential sporting rifles in modern history.",
