    "tabs": {
      "history": {
        "title": "Firearm Origin...",
        "content": "The Beretta 686 Onyx is a break-action over-and-under shotgun that forms part of Beretta’s renowned 680-series, a family of sporting shotguns introduced in the late 20th century. Designed for reliability, balance, and longevity, the 686 platform has become one of the most widely used over/under shotguns in the world.\n\nThe 686 Onyx features a low-profile receiver and dual locking lugs, contributing to a strong and durable action while maintaining a slim, well-balanced feel. The over/under barrel configuration provides a single sighting plane and consistent point of aim, making it especially well suited for clay target sports and upland hunting.\n\nTypically chambered in 12 or 20 gauge, the shotgun uses a break-open action with a selective trigger system, allowing the shooter to choose which barrel fires first. Interchangeable choke tubes provide flexibility in shot pattern, adapting the shotgun to different shooting disciplines and conditions.\n\nBeretta’s reputation for precision manufacturing is evident in the 686’s smooth operation and refined fit and finish. The \"Onyx\" designation often refers to models with a darker, more subdued receiver finish compared to more ornate variants.\n\nThe 686 series is widely used in sporting clays, skeet, and field hunting, valued for its reliability and natural handling characteristics. Its design emphasizes mechanical simplicity and long service life, with many examples remaining in use after decades of regular shooting.\n\nToday, the Beretta 686 Onyx is regarded as a benchmark over/under shotgun, combining traditional craftsmanship with modern engineering in a platform trusted by both recreational shooters and professionals.",
        "wikipedia": {
