    "tabs": {
      "history": {
        "title": "Firearm Origin...",
        "content": "The Remington Model 10 is a pump-action shotgun introduced in 1908 as one of Remington’s earliest successful repeating shotgun designs. Developed by designer John Pedersen, it was notable for several innovative features that distinguished it from other pump-action shotguns of the era.\n\nOne of the Model 10’s most distinctive characteristics is its bottom-loading and bottom-ejecting action. Cartridges are fed into the tubular magazine beneath the barrel and both loaded and ejected through a port on the underside of the receiver. This design helps protect the action from dirt and debris while also making the shotgun suitable for both right- and left-handed shooters.\n\nThe shotgun is typically chambered in 12 gauge and operates using a slide-action mechanism, allowing rapid follow-up shots. Early models featured a takedown design, enabling the barrel and magazine assembly to be separated from the receiver for easier transport and maintenance.\n\nThe Model 10 saw limited military use during World War I, including trench gun variants equipped with bayonet lugs and heat shields. In civilian use, it became popular for hunting and general sporting purposes.\n\nProduction continued until the late 1920s, when it was eventually replaced by the improved Remington Model 29, which refined many aspects of the original design.\n\nToday, the Remington Model 10 is appreciated for its historical significance and unique engineering, representing an early and innovative step in the evolution of pump-action shotguns.",
        "wikipedia": {
          "title": "",
          "content": "",
          "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Springfield_M1903"
        },
        "gunDigest": {
          "title": "",
          "content": "",
          "url": "https://gundigest.com/?s=Springfield%20Armory%20M1903A3%20Gun%20Digest"
        }
      },
